This problem can be approached using graph theory concepts, specifically by considering the problem as a bipartite graph matching problem. Each engineer can be represented as a node, and there will be two sets of nodes: one for the current desks and one for the desired desks. An edge exists between an engineer's current desk and their desired desk.

The problem then reduces to finding the number of perfect matchings in this bipartite graph. This can be computed using the permanent of the adjacency matrix of the graph, which is a generalization of the determinant for non-square matrices. However, for bipartite graphs, the number of perfect matchings can be computed using the permanent modulo a prime number, which is computationally feasible given the constraints.

